Subject: Mark the shared AOT runtime stubs preserve_most

What this does
  Every out-of-line runtime stub the generated code calls -- the step engine,
  the unwinder, the call helpers, the field and element helpers, the epoch and
  site accessors -- is declared and defined `preserve_most`.

Why we need it
  Without it, each call from generated code to a shared stub forces the caller
  to spill every caller-saved register it still needs, which is the main size
  and speed cost of a slow path. `preserve_most` says the stub clobbers almost
  none of them, so live values stay in registers across the call. This is the
  design's main size lever with decision 5 (`E11`).

What it adds
  `QJS_AOT_STUB` in `quickjs-aot-rt.h`, on the declarations of the shared stubs
  and on their definitions. A compiler without `preserve_most` gets an empty
  macro and the build is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

diff --git a/quickjs-aot-rt.h b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
index 994e9de..f3cc452 100644
@@ -10,6 +10,18 @@
 /* The register-frame handle. Opaque here; defined in quickjs.c. */
 typedef struct JSRegRun JSRegRun;
 
+/* The calling convention for the shared runtime stubs the generated code calls
+   on a slow path, an IC miss or a generic operation. Clang's `preserve_most`
+   says the stub clobbers almost no caller-saved registers, so generated code
+   keeps its live values in registers across the call instead of spilling them.
+   Declarations and definitions both use it, so the compiler checks that they
+   agree; without the attribute the two sides still link and run. */
+#if defined(__has_attribute) && __has_attribute(preserve_most)
+#define QJS_AOT_STUB __attribute__((preserve_most))
+#else
+#define QJS_AOT_STUB
+#endif
+
 /* QJS_DRC changes the ownership rule for a register store, so it is part of
    the ABI: a unit built with a different -DQJS_DRC is refused at bind. */
